From dea456b593052a258545493a25038fe6cde3a0b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leopold Li <leopold.lixiaoliang@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 18:09:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] feat(gateway): drop 30s debounce for queue-mode ACK
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem
-------
With the shared _busy_ack_ts 30s cooldown applied to queue ACK, only
the first queued follow-up (B) got its "📥 排队 #1: ..." receipt.
Subsequent follow-ups (C, D, ...) arriving within 30s were silently
suppressed even though each contained distinct content. User could
not tell whether C was received until the drain eventually replied
to it minutes later.

The 30s cooldown was inherited from interrupt/steer ACK where it
serves a real purpose: preventing an "hurry up hurry up hurry up"
spam burst from producing three identical "⚡ Interrupting" bubbles.
That signal is redundant across the burst.

Queue mode is different: B and C are two distinct messages, not two
repeats of one intent. Silencing the second hides receipt information
the user actually needs.

Fix
---
_maybe_send_queue_ack now:
  * Skips the 30s debounce check entirely — every queued message gets
    its own "📥 排队 #N: 'preview...' (Queued)" ACK.
  * Does NOT stamp _busy_ack_ts either, so a queue-mode chatter does
    not silence a later interrupt/steer ACK that carries a different
    intent.

Interrupt/steer mode ACK debounce is unchanged.

Tests
-----
test_busy_text_mode_queue_sends_ack_by_default rewritten:
  * Sends two follow-ups back-to-back.
  * Simulates the caller enqueueing the first between calls so the
    second sees queue_depth=1.
  * Asserts BOTH ACKs fired (was: only first fired due to debounce).
  * Asserts positions increment: first #1, second #2.
  * Asserts previews carry each message's own text.

Full suite: 50/50 pass.
